Filetype: Flipper RFID key
Version: 1
Frequency: 125000
Bit Count: 64
Key type: EM4100
Key: DE AD BE EF CA
Data: DE AD BE EF CA

# ==============================================================
#  Audit_Trigger_Badge.rfid
#
#  Defensive-use RFID emulation. The UID `DE AD BE EF CA`
#  is a deliberately impossible pattern -- the byte sequence
#  `DE AD BE EF CA` cannot appear on a real-world EM4100 wafer,
#  so this file CANNOT impersonate any access-control badge.
#
#  Twist vs. a vanilla cloned credential: this file is meant
#  to be EMULATED by the Flipper at a desk-mounted RFID reader
#  (or near an attached USB HF/LF reader like ACR122U) to
#  trigger a known "audit mode" event. BugReaperX detects
#  the impossible UID and reports:
#    "Audit event: synthetic UID detected (DEADBEEF CA)"
#  which is useful for:
#    - Validating that an RFID-enabled workstation can detect
#      emulated input at all.
#    - Pen-test verification that the Flipper's RFID emulator
#      is wired correctly (probe + reader handshake).
#    - Smoke testing RFID log pipelines end-to-end without
#      touching anyone else's badges.
#
#  EM4100 protocol summary:
#    - 125 kHz carrier, ASK/OOK modulation.
#    - 64-bit Manchester-coded transmission:
#      10 sync + 8 version + 32 data (the 5 bytes above) +
#      14 parity + 4 stop = approximately 64 bits.
#    - `Key:` / `Data:` fields below encode just the 40 data
#      bits; the Flipper rebuilds the rest on the wire.
#
#  Format note: modern firmware reads `Key type` + `Data`;
#  the `Key:` line is kept for older firmware and the
#  workspace validator, which both expect it.
# ==============================================================

# Verify with:  python ..\scripts\verify_flipper_files.py
# Data tests:    python ..\scripts\test_flipper_payloads.py
